    "group": "task",
    "section": "SCORING RUBRIC — seven transparent weighted axes",
    "text": "SCORING RUBRIC — score TRANSPARENTLY against seven weighted axes (each 0-100); the overall `score` is\ntheir weighted average, and your `report` MUST include the per-axis breakdown (a small table: axis / score /\none-line reason) so the author and the public see exactly how the number was derived. The weights:\n  • Reproducibility & correctness (weight 30) — compiles, the code runs from a blank environment, and every\n    claim/number/figure regenerates from the open data correctly. The journal's core: work that does not\n    reproduce cannot score this highly and cannot be accepted.\n  • Honesty & identification (15) — claims match the evidence; in-sample/descriptive results are not dressed\n    as causal/predictive; competing explanations are confronted; limitations are candid; data described truthfully.\n  • Communication & clarity (15) — informative title (matching the metadata), plain-language abstract, clear\n    structure and argument, precise prose, the key takeaway surfaced.\n  • Visualisation (12) — publication-grade, code-generated figures: right chart type, axes labelled with units,\n    self-contained captions, faithfully showing ALL the data the captions claim.\n  • Accessibility (8) — figures readable in greyscale / for colour-vision deficiency (class encoded by line\n    style + marker, not colour alone), plain-language abstract, SI units.\n  • References (10) — every citation real and individually DOI-verified, correctly cited, no orphans/undefined.\n  • Significance & rigour (10) — the question matters, the method is sound, and uncertainty is quantified.\nPut the seven sub-scores in the `scores` object below, give the weighted total as `score`, and end the report\nwith one line stating your CONFIDENCE in the verdict and any caveat. The rubric is PUBLIC — be consistent and fair.",
    "why": "The public, weighted rubric every score is built from: Reproducibility & correctness (30), Honesty & identification (15), Communication & clarity (15), Visualisation (12), Accessibility (8), References (10), Significance & rigour (10). The overall score is their weighted average, and the report must include the per-axis breakdown — and the seven sub-scores are persisted in the verdict's `scores` object, so EVERY published review now shows the transparent per-axis bars to author and public (see a paper's review rounds). Making the rubric public is the transparency guarantee — the reviewer is bound to be consistent and fair because the workings are shown."
